1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:01,266
Check this out!

2
00:00:01,533 --> 00:00:07,400
I actually ran weather forecast calculations
using the latest AI weather prediction model!

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:12,133
And AI-based weather forecasting can be computed in just a few minutes!

4
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:18,666
Compared to traditional numerical weather prediction,
it's much faster and cheaper to compute!

5
00:00:19,133 --> 00:00:25,333
I actually tried it myself,
and it only cost about 500 yen!

6
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:32,933
It's surprising that you can do weather forecasting
for less than a cup of coffee or a McDonald's combo meal

7
00:00:33,466 --> 00:00:39,866
Today I'll introduce the science of weather forecasting
and the latest AI weather prediction technology

8
00:00:42,633 --> 00:00:48,266
Do you know how daily weather forecasts
are actually created?

9
00:00:48,766 --> 00:00:52,633
It actually involves incredibly complex calculations

10
00:00:53,033 --> 00:01:02,466
The entire Earth's atmosphere and water, air flow, temperature, pressure,
and how clouds and water vapor change over time

11
00:01:02,566 --> 00:01:06,066
are simulated based on physics equations

12
00:01:06,666 --> 00:01:16,300
Solving fluid dynamics and thermodynamics equations on a global scale requires extremely complex and intricate calculations

13
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:20,733
When you try to run these calculations on a computer,

14
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:29,266
it takes several hours to produce a 10-day forecast using a supercomputer made up of hundreds of machines

15
00:01:29,833 --> 00:01:32,600
That's how computationally intensive it is

16
00:01:33,033 --> 00:01:37,866
It would be nearly impossible for ordinary companies or individuals to do the same...

17
00:01:38,266 --> 00:01:40,266
That was the story until recently

18
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:49,600
As I actually demonstrated, weather forecasting is now becoming possible for about the cost of a cup of coffee

19
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:56,266
What dramatically changed the situation in recent years is the advancement of AI technology

20
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:07,933
AI-based weather prediction models have emerged, and a 10-day forecast can be computed in just 1 minute using a single high-performance GPU

21
00:02:08,866 --> 00:02:12,800
What used to take hundreds of machines and several hours now takes one GPU and one minute

22
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,300
It's surprisingly fast, isn't it?

23
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:23,033
And the good news is that some of these pre-trained models
are publicly available

24
00:02:23,433 --> 00:02:29,866
If you rent a high-performance GPU in the cloud,
even individuals can easily run them

25
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,433
Before talking about AI weather forecasting,

26
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:37,433
let's first take a closer look at how traditional weather forecasts are made

27
00:02:37,733 --> 00:02:40,033
Let's examine this in more detail

28
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:46,333
Air moves, temperature changes, water vapor becomes clouds and rain falls

29
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,500
These phenomena are known to be describable by

30
00:02:52,066 --> 00:02:56,233
various differential equations including fluid dynamics and thermodynamics

31
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,766
You might think "well, just solve those equations then"

32
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:08,633
Unfortunately, these equations are too complex
to solve with paper and pencil

33
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:13,133
Unlike the quadratic equations you learn in high school, there's no formula

34
00:03:13,466 --> 00:03:17,900
so you can't get an answer in the form of "x equals something"

35
00:03:18,533 --> 00:03:22,400
This is where numerical calculation comes in

36
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:27,733
You might find it easier to think of it as simulation

37
00:03:28,233 --> 00:03:35,566
In numerical calculation, we approximate by
connecting points on a curve with lines

38
00:03:36,033 --> 00:03:41,733
It's not the exact answer, but the idea is
to get as close to the correct value as possible

39
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:48,033
Let me briefly explain what numerical calculation does

40
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:50,500
First, we set a starting point

41
00:03:51,233 --> 00:03:55,633
Let's say when x is 0, f(x) equals 1

42
00:03:56,366 --> 00:04:02,666
Then we calculate the value just a little ahead,
when x is 0.1

43
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,433
The differential equation tells us the slope,
so we move forward a bit following that slope

44
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,266
Then we calculate the next point, when x is 0.2

45
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:20,900
By repeating this process,
the shape of the function gradually becomes visible

46
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:28,266
One of these step-by-step methods
is called the "Runge-Kutta method"

47
00:04:28,666 --> 00:04:32,533
The important question here is "how finely do we divide the steps?"

48
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:37,866
In the earlier example, we calculated in 0.1 increments

49
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:46,700
If you want more precision,
you need to make it finer: 0.01, 0.001, and so on

50
00:04:47,166 --> 00:04:53,466
But if you make the steps 10 times smaller,
the number of calculations increases 10 times

51
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,300
The important issue is that making divisions finer
causes computational costs to explode

52
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:05,333
Weather forecasting requires simulating the entire Earth

53
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:10,033
The earlier example was 1-dimensional, but the real atmosphere is 3-dimensional

54
00:05:10,833 --> 00:05:16,000
Space extends in east-west, north-south, and altitude directions

55
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:18,100
And time is added on top of that

56
00:05:19,033 --> 00:05:25,933
For example, dividing the Earth into 25km square grids
results in about 1 million points

57
00:05:26,366 --> 00:05:29,500
Plus dozens of layers in the altitude direction

58
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,900
And time is also divided finely and advanced step by step

59
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:44,700
So what was 100 calculations in 1-dimension
explodes exponentially in 3-dimensions plus time

60
00:05:45,366 --> 00:05:49,800
At this point, regular computers can no longer handle it

61
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:55,366
This is why weather forecasting requires supercomputers

62
00:05:56,266 --> 00:06:01,633
That's why cutting-edge weather forecasting
becomes a national-project-level endeavor

63
00:06:02,633 --> 00:06:10,433
But then came today's main topic:
a completely new approach using AI

64
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:15,133
Here's roughly what machine learning does

65
00:06:15,733 --> 00:06:18,233
First, prepare a large amount of data

66
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:23,866
Use that data to predict what output comes from an input

67
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:32,266
Calculate how much the prediction differs from the correct answer,
and adjust the model to reduce that difference

68
00:06:32,733 --> 00:06:39,366
By repeating this many, many times,
predictions gradually get closer to the correct answers

69
00:06:39,733 --> 00:06:49,066
Instead of humans saying "calculate using these rules,"
the machine finds patterns from the data on its own

70
00:06:49,533 --> 00:06:53,833
This is the basic concept of machine learning

71
00:06:53,966 --> 00:06:56,500
Let's look at an extremely simple example

72
00:06:56,833 --> 00:07:02,666
I created an AI model that predicts
the parabolic trajectory of a thrown ball

73
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:08,900
The training data consists of trajectories
from various initial velocities and angles

74
00:07:09,433 --> 00:07:13,233
They were simply generated using quadratic function calculations

75
00:07:13,733 --> 00:07:16,033
When you train the model with this data,

76
00:07:16,833 --> 00:07:23,033
it can predict trajectories
just by inputting the initial velocity and launch angle

77
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,500
Here's the result of actually running it

78
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,466
It predicts the projectile motion quite accurately, doesn't it?

79
00:07:32,133 --> 00:07:36,500
The interesting thing is that this model doesn't know the quadratic equation

80
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:43,000
But by seeing lots of data about
"when thrown this way, it flew like that"

81
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:48,200
it learns the patterns
and becomes able to predict accurate trajectories

82
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,000
This is similar to when we throw trash into a trash can

83
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:57,933
Without thinking about physics formulas, through experience

84
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:03,533
we just know "if I throw with this much force at this angle, it'll go in"

85
00:08:04,333 --> 00:08:09,733
It's not good manners though, so please make sure
to properly dispose of your trash without throwing it

86
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:12,566
To be honest though,

87
00:08:12,933 --> 00:08:19,566
there's frankly no advantage
to using machine learning for predicting projectile motion

88
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:25,100
Because you can get the answer immediately by solving a quadratic equation

89
00:08:26,366 --> 00:08:31,700
But this simple example of having an AI model predict projectile motion

90
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:37,633
leads to predicting complex physical phenomena
like weather forecasting

91
00:08:38,466 --> 00:08:44,533
A key feature of machine learning models is that training and prediction costs differ greatly

92
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:51,700
Training, the stage of learning patterns from data,
is indeed intensive

93
00:08:52,633 --> 00:09:01,133
Reading in decades of meteorological observation data and numerical forecast data,
and training thousands, tens of thousands of times

94
00:09:01,766 --> 00:09:07,700
But once training is complete and the model is built,
prediction is nearly instantaneous

95
00:09:08,333 --> 00:09:16,266
When you input the current atmospheric state,
it outputs the state 6 hours later based on learned patterns

96
00:09:17,033 --> 00:09:24,366
There's no need to advance a little, calculate again,
and accumulate small steps like numerical calculation

97
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:29,600
The traditional method calculates from scratch every time

98
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:36,300
Set up physics equations, do numerical calculation,
and steadily accumulate step by step

99
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:42,133
The AI model is like having solved many past exams
and grasped the patterns

100
00:09:42,766 --> 00:09:45,600
For tests, rather than memorizing detailed principles,

101
00:09:45,933 --> 00:09:51,400
just memorizing how to solve past problems
is indeed an effective method

102
00:09:52,033 --> 00:09:59,400
"Okay it's fast, but what about accuracy?"
This is a natural question that comes up

103
00:10:00,133 --> 00:10:05,100
Actually, the latest AI models achieve accuracy equal to traditional numerical forecasting

104
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:09,700
and in some cases, even better

105
00:10:10,433 --> 00:10:14,133
For example, Hurricane Lee in 2023

106
00:10:14,633 --> 00:10:23,200
A major hurricane that formed in the Atlantic and
caused significant damage to the Atlantic coasts of the US and Canada

107
00:10:23,666 --> 00:10:26,400
Where would this hurricane make landfall?

108
00:10:27,233 --> 00:10:33,566
The AI model developed by Google DeepMind
accurately predicted it 9 days in advance

109
00:10:34,333 --> 00:10:42,066
Traditional forecasting could only be confident 6 days ahead,
so warnings could be issued 3 days earlier

110
00:10:42,866 --> 00:10:48,133
"If AI is this fast, do we even need supercomputers anymore?"

111
00:10:48,533 --> 00:10:52,400
Some might think so,
but that's actually not the case

112
00:10:53,133 --> 00:10:55,700
The data that AI models learn from

113
00:10:56,333 --> 00:10:58,500
Where does it come from?

114
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:02,500
It comes from traditional numerical weather prediction

115
00:11:03,233 --> 00:11:05,833
Gathering meteorological observation data from around the world

116
00:11:06,366 --> 00:11:09,633
Physical simulations computed by supercomputers

117
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:16,300
AI models learn from data that recreates
what the past atmosphere was like

118
00:11:17,233 --> 00:11:25,433
In other words, AI models don't replace physical simulation,
they learn from its results

119
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,066
AI models alone cannot produce highly accurate weather forecasts

120
00:11:31,633 --> 00:11:37,100
So far I've explained how AI-based weather forecasting works

121
00:11:37,733 --> 00:11:43,966
But you can't really believe it
until you try it yourself, right?

122
00:11:44,533 --> 00:11:47,133
So let's actually do it!

123
00:11:47,733 --> 00:11:55,466
This time I'm using FourCastNet,
an AI weather prediction model released by NVIDIA

124
00:11:56,133 --> 00:12:02,666
The pre-trained model is freely available
and anyone can download and use it

125
00:12:03,366 --> 00:12:07,533
However, you need a high-performance GPU to run it

126
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,500
Regular PC GPUs aren't quite powerful enough

127
00:12:12,833 --> 00:12:19,766
So this time I rented cloud-based GPUs
by the hour from a service called RunPod

128
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:23,766
I rented an A100 GPU

129
00:12:24,466 --> 00:12:28,433
The hourly rate is about $1.50

130
00:12:28,933 --> 00:12:32,400
That's about 235 yen in Japanese currency

131
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:38,600
Using the latest H100 GPU would be faster,

132
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:46,800
but it costs $2.70 per hour,
so I chose the A100 considering cost balance

133
00:12:47,333 --> 00:12:55,000
For input, I used global meteorological observation data
freely published by NOAA, the US weather agency

134
00:12:55,300 --> 00:13:04,200
I downloaded actual data from January 1, 2025 at 00:00 UTC
and used it as input data

135
00:13:04,933 --> 00:13:12,700
From there, I decided to predict 60 hours ahead,
until 12:00 on January 3rd, in 6-hour intervals

136
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:18,833
The inference for the 3-day weather forecast
took about 35 minutes

137
00:13:19,433 --> 00:13:27,400
Including environment setup, downloading meteorological data, and loading the model, the total cost was about $3

138
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,333
Just under 500 yen

139
00:13:29,966 --> 00:13:32,700
I could do weather forecasting for less than a single coin!

140
00:13:33,766 --> 00:13:39,466
Let's look at the results
This is the weather predicted by the AI

141
00:13:40,233 --> 00:13:51,233
Surface temperature, wind speed, sea level pressure, upper atmospheric pressure patterns,
I could predict how various meteorological elements change every 6 hours

142
00:13:51,833 --> 00:13:56,366
This kind of calculation is now possible
from a personal budget, for less than 500 yen

143
00:13:56,833 --> 00:13:59,966
This was unthinkable just 5 years ago

144
00:14:00,266 --> 00:14:10,233
However, I haven't rigorously verified whether it matched
the actual weather from January 1-3, 2025

145
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:17,733
Please consider this just a demo
of running an AI model and getting prediction results

146
00:14:18,333 --> 00:14:26,033
Still, what used to require national-project-level
supercomputer resources just a few years ago

147
00:14:26,466 --> 00:14:29,733
can now be done for the cost of a cup of coffee

148
00:14:30,266 --> 00:14:33,066
I think this is truly groundbreaking

149
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:39,500
When I rented the GPU on RunPod
to make this video,

150
00:14:39,766 --> 00:14:43,500
I thought even if it ended up being
"I spent $10 and got nothing!"

151
00:14:43,866 --> 00:14:45,600
that kind of result

152
00:14:45,866 --> 00:14:50,966
would still make good content,
which would be nice

153
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:53,633
But it worked more easily than expected

154
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,233
So I decided to emphasize the
"under 500 yen" angle

155
00:14:58,933 --> 00:15:03,266
I thought that was good content too, so it all worked out

156
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:11,233
Besides NVIDIA's model that I used,
AI weather forecasting research is advancing worldwide

157
00:15:11,766 --> 00:15:13,166
Google's GraphCast

158
00:15:13,833 --> 00:15:15,466
Microsoft's Aurora

159
00:15:16,233 --> 00:15:18,100
Huawei's Pangu-Weather

160
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:22,733
And ECMWF's AIFS

161
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:29,833
Not just major IT companies,
but traditional meteorological agencies are also adopting AI

162
00:15:30,333 --> 00:15:38,033
Also, rather than replacing everything with AI,
various hybrid approaches are emerging

163
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:41,733
For example, in traditional numerical forecasting,

164
00:15:42,033 --> 00:15:45,833
replacing only the computationally heavy parts with AI

165
00:15:46,566 --> 00:15:49,866
or improving observation data accuracy with AI

166
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:54,233
or correcting numerical forecast results with AI

167
00:15:55,100 --> 00:16:01,666
Research combining the best of both AI
and traditional numerical forecasting is advancing in various directions

168
00:16:02,266 --> 00:16:06,833
But as you all know, AI is not omnipotent

169
00:16:07,966 --> 00:16:13,100
Large language models like ChatGPT
sometimes tell plausible lies

170
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:17,966
Image generation AI draws people eating ramen with their bare hands

171
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:23,633
You're all well aware that AI does strange things

172
00:16:24,566 --> 00:16:28,800
AI is very smart and useful, but not omnipotent

173
00:16:29,466 --> 00:16:31,966
Weather forecasting AI is the same

174
00:16:33,033 --> 00:16:38,366
That's why we need to think about how to incorporate AI technology into scientific research

175
00:16:38,933 --> 00:16:41,166
Researchers and engineers around the world

176
00:16:41,633 --> 00:16:47,933
and we who use the results of their research
need to think about this

177
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:58,000
Weather forecasting AI is a particularly important example
that's closely connected to our daily lives

178
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:00,866
Just one note at the end

179
00:17:01,833 --> 00:17:07,833
In Japan, you need permission from the Japan Meteorological Agency
to conduct weather forecasting as a business

180
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:12,900
It's fine if you're just trying it out for yourself like this time

181
00:17:13,366 --> 00:17:18,533
But if you start a business using this,
you might be violating the law

182
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:23,400
Please enjoy this only for research and learning purposes

183
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:31,633
Today I gave a brief explanation of numerical and AI weather forecasting

184
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:36,800
And actually ran an AI weather forecasting model

185
00:17:37,533 --> 00:17:40,500
Weather forecasting that used to require supercomputers

186
00:17:41,033 --> 00:17:45,233
can now be easily tried by anyone for less than 500 yen

187
00:17:46,233 --> 00:17:54,366
Instead of going to Starbucks for coffee once,
it might be fun to play with weather forecasting

188
00:17:55,033 --> 00:17:58,766
See you in the next video, have a good day!
